                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Correct. The titration of a strong base produces water, and the solution is neutral at the equivalence point."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "cbe896b4423f4799b22301718e900f94",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1691201299"
              },
              "id": "3288720195",
              "legacyMatch": "d82edf234d39426c8bc1b446159c60e9",
              "rule": "input like {d82edf234d39426c8bc1b446159c60e9}",
              "score": 10
            },
            {
              "feedback": {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Incorrect. Remember that at the equivalence point the number of moles acid and moles of base are equal, and the pH at the equivalence point depends on the type of titration. For a titration involving a strong acid and strong base, the moles of hydronium and hydroxide react stoichiometrically 1:1 with each other to become water, and there are no leftover moles of hydronium or hydroxide. So the solution is neutral at the equivalence point."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "d2fbcdbd91454900808213722e410e43",
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "For a titration involving a weak acid and strong base (or weak base and strong acid), the moles of the strong component react stoichiometrically with the weak acid (HA) or weak base (B), which neutralizes the weak component and converts the weak component into its conjugate base (A"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "-"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ") or conjugate acid (BH"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "+"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "). The presence of the conjugate acid or base at the equivalence point means the solution will not be neutral at the equivalence point. The solution is basic in the presence of a conjugate base (A"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "-"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ") and acidic in the presence of a conjugate acid (BH"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "+"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ")."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "f66c18066a8649ca87f205503cdc4c65",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "151747268"
